Production Checklist Before You Call It “Live”
Video 5 Script (same tone and structure as Video 1)

Today I’m going to show you what I do before I call something live.

Not when it “looks fine.”
Not when it “probably works.”

When it is actually ready.

We are going to run a simple production checklist.
No theory.
No lecturing.
Just execution.

Here is what we are going to do.
We will build locally.
We will check logs.
We will verify environment variables.
We will test an API route.
We will test the database.
And we will confirm the deployment in Vercel.

The tools are the same as before.
Your local machine.
Antigravity.
GitHub.
Vercel.
Supabase.

I assume you already have the app working.
Database connected.
Environment variables configured.
And a successful deployment.

My name is Steve.
I was a professional software tester for over 12 years.
My job was to verify that systems behave correctly under real conditions.
So this is not superstition.
This is discipline.

Let’s start.

First step.
Build locally in production mode.

Not dev mode.
Production mode.

Run the production build.
If it fails locally, it will fail in Vercel.
Better to catch it here.

If the build passes, good.
If not, read the error.
Fix it before you push.

Second step.
Check the logs.

Run the app locally in production mode.
Open the terminal.
Watch for warnings.
Watch for undefined variables.
Watch for silent errors.

No red text.
No weird output.
Clean console.

Third step.
Verify environment variables.

Open your local environment file.
Confirm names are correct.
No typos.
No missing values.

Then go to Vercel project settings.
Compare them.
Same names.
Same keys.
No accidental differences.

Fourth step.
Test an API route directly.

Open your browser.
Go to your live domain.
Add /api/ping or whatever route you created.

You want a clean JSON response.
No HTML error page.
No 500.
No undefined.

If the route works directly, that’s a good sign.

Fifth step.
Test the database.

Load the page that fetches real data.
Confirm data is rendered.
Then change something in the database.
Refresh.

Does it reflect the change?
If yes, your connection is real.
Not cached.
Not fake.

Final step.
Confirm the deployment itself.

Go to Vercel.
Open the latest deployment.
Check the commit hash.
Confirm it matches the latest commit in GitHub.

No ghost builds.
No outdated version.

Now refresh the live site one more time.

Everything loads.
No console errors.
No broken routes.
Database responding.
Deployment clean.

Now you can call it live.

Not because it feels done.
But because you verified it.

This is pure systems energy.

Small checks.
Clear loop.
No drama.

Once this checklist becomes automatic, you stop hoping things work.
You know they work.

In the next video, we’ll move from “it works” to “it’s structured properly.”

See you there.